            <ArticleTitle>Sleeve gastrectomy: have we finally found the holy grail of bariatric surgery? A review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>4930-4942</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">11859</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy has become one of the most commonly performed bariatric operations. It is essentially a restrictive bariatric operation; however, a series of hormonal changes occurring postoperatively contribute to decreased appetite and reduced food intake.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This is a literature review of recent articles published on Pubmed, Medline and Google Scholar databases in English.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Although, laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy is commonly performed worldwide, there is still a lack of standardization regarding the surgical technique. Standardizing the surgical technique is essential in order to minimize postoperative complications and offer patients the best long-term weight loss.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Laparoscopic sleeve gastrectomy appears to be an effective bariatric operation. It is relatively easy to perform, well tolerated by the patients and very effective regarding long-term excessive weight loss and resolution of the comorbidities, with minimum nutritional deficiencies.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
